the action that is taking place  in the east of the country i mean boris  johnson himself  has already expressed concern for the  donbass region i mean is there now an  inevitability  to the russians taking territory in that  part of the country  i don't think there's an inevitability  at all i mean i think the last time that  i had the privilege of uh speaking for  the government in the morning was about  two or three weeks ago and we were  reflecting then on how uh imminent the  demise of mario paul may be and yet  those fighters that are in mario paul  defending it so heroically are still  going through slater and they are  soaking up a huge amount of russian  combat power as that defense continues  so um i think that what we will see in  the donbass is a very different type of  conflict to the one that we saw uh  around kiev i think we'll see a conflict  between two forces that are much more  evenly balanced uh where the ukrainians  have the advantage of defensive  positions that have been  dug in and prepared over the last eight  years and that's going to make it an  extraordinarily difficult nut for the  russians to crack  and i think that with all of the support  that the ukrainians are getting from  around the world there's every chance  that the ukrainians can see them off  we know that the ukrainians certainly  have been fighting and fighting hard  your boss ben wallace was briefing mps  yesterday and viewers can see it on the  screen perhaps as many as 15 000 russian  personnel have been killed perhaps as  many as 30 of the battalion tactical  groups  yeah i mean that's right those are the  numbers that the ukrainians report and  that is and that's vast that is nearly  three times as many uh  dead as russia suffered in the first  chechen war  and  and that is  that is  nobody should rejoice in that that's 15  000 russian families who haven't got  their son or husband or brother coming  home  but  people in russia should be clear that  that is because  the russian plan has been  defined by incredible hubris massive  political interference in military plan  making and we're seeing exactly the same  now  where there is pressure from moscow to  deliver a victory by the 9th of may so  that putin can have a nice parade  through red square  even though that makes no military sense  the weather in the donbass over the last  week or so has been absolutely awful and  so driving the russian army to launch an  offensive now simply so putin can have a  nice day in the sun on may the 9th is  just going to cost russia thousands more  lives now if you're on the side of the  ukrainians and you want the ukrainians  to succeed in defending their territory  that russian hubris is a massive massive  advantage for ukraine but if i was a  russian family i'd be deeply concerned  that those in the kremlin were not  acting in my interests indeed  on that point then minister what what  more can you tell us about the  additional assistance that we're  providing a lot of kind of speculation  perhaps informed speculation in the  papers about storm or vehicles being  sent perhaps as uh providing some form  of radar as well what more can you tell  us on that  well that's exactly right and you know  the secretary of state has been very  very clear that russia only has itself  to blame because every time we have gone  up a gear in terms of what we are  providing it is a direct consequence of  the way that russia has conducted its  operations  in ukraine  now stormer is a air defense facility  that is mounted on an armored vehicle we  think that that's necessary uh in order  to  give greater protection to ukrainian  ground forces and the civilian  populations in some cities  we have been scouring the earth for  uh 152 millimeter artillery ammunition  which is uh the artillery ammunition  that the ukrainians would need for their  existing guns um but we've also now been  working with allies around the world to  provide them with uh 155 millimeter  which is the nato standard caliber  and that means that hundreds of  thousands more artillery rounds are now  flowing in from the united states  particularly to ukraine there are  countries in eastern europe that are  giving up sophisticated air defense  systems hundreds of tanks um i mean this  is an amazing effort and the uk has been  to the fore in corralling it  the the response though from the  russians in particular then the foreign  minister sergey lavrov  warning that  this could be as on the precipice of  world war three already suggesting that  russia is not fighting ukraine it's it's  fighting nato i mean obviously that  clearly removes you know the ukrainians  agency and all of this but i wonder what  you make of the at the brinksmanship in  terms of the rhetoric about potentially  your third global conflict  well look i mean there's a couple of  points of distinction firstly the donor  community is not nato nato uh has been  involved in uh reinforcing its eastern  flank so a lot of the deployments of our  troops ships and aircraft to eastern  europe are very much under the nato flag  but the donor effort is something that  is being brought together by countries  that are from yes many of them are from  nato but others are from beyond the  australians the kiwis the canadians uh  sorry the canadians are in nato um and  and so you know that is uh it's not nato  that is doing the the the military aid  but sergey lavrov might also reflect  that the reason that there is a war in  ukraine right now is because russia  rolled over the borders of a sovereign  country and started to invade their  territory nato is not an expansionist  offensive alliance nato is a defensive  alliance and  all of this noise from moscow about  somehow  uh their their attack on ukraine being a  response to nato aggression it's just  utter utter nonsense  um minister i wonder if we can move away  from from from your brief now to perhaps  the kind of the biggest individual issue  facing your politics and indeed the  public domestically the the cost of  living we understand that there is to be  a cost of living cabinet today the prime  minister very keen to get as many ideas  as possible you know for as little money  as possible given what we've been  spending over the past few years  what is the hope from this meeting this  morning  well look i mean the reason that the  meeting is happening and the reason that  so many meetings on the cost of living  happen in government every single day  is because the moment that you go out  and knock on doors in your constituency  what you find  is that that is the thing that  hard-working families across the united  kingdom are most concerned about the  cost of living is getting to such a  point now where even people on good  wages are struggling to make ends meet  and they are looking to the government  to help them with solutions now what  they were deciding cabinet this morning  is for them and it's certainly not for  me to to try and trail but already for  this year alone the government's put 22  billion pounds of targeted funding in to  support those who are struggling most  with the cost of living and the fact  that cabinet is meeting today to discuss  it more shows you just how seriously  this is taken by the prime minister the  chancellor and the rest of their  colleagues and cabinets but it isn't the  simple truth though i mean i've read the  article that the the sun was briefed on  on the cabinet meeting taking place this  morning and the kind of the one of the  biggest announcement one of the biggest  policies that was was flagged up in the  article was removing tariffs on rice now  i understand that that will help you  know but we're talking pennies and not  pounds i mean isn't the simple truth  that there's not an awful lot that the  government can do without spending money  well i mean  there's no doubt about it that these  interventions are expensive 22 billion  pounds of government intervention to  assist in uh to assist with families  with their cost of living is already  huge there'll be other levers that  government can pull and you know  not no one of them will be a golden  bullet but cumulatively if you can  provide people with lots of small  savings across their cost of living then  cumulatively that does start to make a  difference so i wouldn't  i wouldn't sniff at the removal of a  tariff on a particular food type because  if that sits alongside  a resistance a reduction in  fuel duty at the pumps which has  happened if that sits alongside a rising  of the personal allowance an increase in  the national living wage you know  cumulatively you can start to see and  you know and assisting people with their  energy bills cumulative you can start to  see how that starts to ease the pressure  on the families who are struggling the  most minister i i promise you i am not  being sniffy about cheaper rice you have  no idea how much curry i eat um i do  however want us to conclude on just  on a couple of related topics around  around freedom of speech um angela  raynor of course you will by now have  seen that the article such as it was in  the mail on sunday i i have to admit i  feel slightly uncomfortable this morning  yesterday clearly we were talking with  your colleague chris phil the article  itself misogynistic sexist and all the  rest of it and then we hear that the  speaker of the house of commons has  summoned the mail on sunday's editor for  a meeting presumably to chastise him  does that sit easily with you you know  the speaker of the house of commons  summoning a journalist to explain their  actions  well look  lindsay is fiercely independent and  rightly so uh you know he is the speaker  of the house of commons and he must do  what he thinks is in parliament's  interest i would just reflect that you  know i don't think that the speaker is  trying to limit freedom of speech in  fact my understanding was i wasn't in  the chamber yesterday to hear him say it  firsthand my understanding is that he  made the point of saying that he wants  to protect freedom of speech and that  the role of the press  lobby in parliament is hugely important  um but there are female colleagues  in the house of commons on all sides of  the house  who have to put up with a level of  sexism misogyny every day that frankly  is unfair uh it is a disadvantage to  them that  i don't have to face as a male member of  parliament or a male minister and i just  can't believe that in 2022 we're in a  place where  the story that was written on sunday was  was written i can't believe that one of  my colleagues the story was written  because your colleagues i think it's  despicable  and i i i just find it outrageous that  that colleague who who sits within a  party that has delivered two female  prime ministers that has two women in  the great offices of state that is  surrounded by amazingly capable female  mps on the conservative benches as well  as over on the labour benches too i just  i just don't know what parliament that  they think they sit in i just i find the  whole thing ridiculous offensive i'm  sorry for angela um and i you know if  the speaker is going to put a shot  across the bowels about the way that  women in parliament are being reported  that's a good thing i have no doubt that  he is  protecting freedom of speech and he  won't want to see that impringed but i  do think that westminster has just got  itself into a mess and it it looks awful  yeah  to continue with the freedom of speech  um theme what what are your thoughts  about the the taking private of uh  social media platform uh twitter by elon  musk i mean the bills certainly aren't  there and the board obviously still has  to agree but do you have any concerns  about the richest man in the world  owning perhaps one of the most  significant social media platforms  well i i the government doesn't have a  view the government will let this go  through as as a as a transaction um but  there is  um i think you know elon musk has made  some good noises about being a protector  of freedom of speech i think i've also  heard him say some stuff about uh ending  the anonymity of the trolls on on  twitter  and you know those of us who use twitter  as part of our jobs  will know that twitter can be an  absolute sewer sometimes because people  do have anonymity and that makes them  very brave uh when they have anonymity  um i if twitter is going to be cleaned  up as a platform uh be  somewhere where freedom of speech is  championed um but the anonymous  anonymous abuse becomes uh more  difficult then that's a good thing but  that's my view and the government's view  is that this is a this is a takeover bit  on which we have no view at the moment  jcp great to have you on the program as  always thanks for joining us thanks now  thank you

